<p class="gna-lede">A jaw cyst is a fluid-filled sac that grows slowly inside the jawbone. Most are benign and many cause no symptoms at all, which is why they are often first spotted on a routine dental X-ray. Treatment usually means removing the cyst surgically — either in one stage (enucleation) or by shrinking it first (marsupialisation) — followed by a period of X-ray checks to make sure it has not come back.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-a-jaw-cyst">What is a jaw cyst?</h2>
<p>A cyst is a closed pocket lined by a thin layer of cells (the epithelium) and filled with fluid or soft material. In the jaws, most cysts are <strong>odontogenic</strong>, meaning they arise from leftover cells that once formed the teeth. A smaller group are <strong>non-odontogenic</strong> and develop from other tissues — for example, the nasopalatine duct cyst behind the upper front teeth.</p>
<p>Because the lining keeps producing fluid, pressure inside a cyst rises slowly and the surrounding bone gradually remodels around it. This is why jaw cysts tend to grow over months or years rather than days, and why a large cyst can thin the bone and push teeth out of position before anyone notices.</p>
<p>It is also worth knowing what a jaw cyst is <em>not</em>. Several conditions can look like a cyst on an X-ray, including benign tumours such as <a href="/blogs/ameloblastoma-jaw-tumour-explained/">ameloblastoma</a> and, rarely, malignant ones. That is why the lining removed at surgery is always sent for laboratory examination.</p>

<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Most jaw cysts are benign and grow slowly; many are found by chance on a dental X-ray.</li>
    <li>The three types surgeons see most often are radicular, dentigerous and odontogenic keratocyst.</li>
    <li>Enucleation removes the whole cyst in one operation; marsupialisation shrinks a large cyst first.</li>
    <li>Every cyst lining should be examined under a microscope to confirm the diagnosis.</li>
    <li>Follow-up X-rays are part of treatment — some types, particularly keratocysts, can recur years later.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="symptoms">What symptoms do jaw cysts cause?</h2>
<p>Small cysts usually cause no symptoms. As a cyst enlarges, or if it becomes infected, you may notice:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A slow, firm swelling</strong> of the jaw or gum, sometimes noticed only as a change in the shape of the face or a denture that no longer fits.</li>
  <li><strong>Teeth moving, tilting or becoming loose</strong> without obvious gum disease.</li>
  <li><strong>A tooth that has not come through</strong> — for example, a wisdom tooth or an upper canine that remains buried in the bone.</li>
  <li><strong>Pain, tenderness or discharge</strong> with a salty taste, which usually means the cyst has become infected.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness or tingling</strong> of the lower lip or chin, caused by pressure on the nerve that runs through the lower jaw.</li>
</ul>
<p>None of these signs is specific to cysts. Jaw swelling can also come from a dental abscess, a salivary gland problem or a tumour, and our guide to the <a href="/blogs/what-are-the-symptoms-of-jaw-disease/">symptoms of jaw disease</a> covers the wider picture. Numbness of the lip in particular should never be ignored — it needs prompt specialist assessment.</p>

<h2 id="types">The three most common types</h2>
<h3>Radicular (periapical) cyst</h3>
<p>This is the most common jaw cyst. It forms at the tip of the root of a tooth whose nerve has died — usually after deep decay, a large old filling or an injury. Long-standing inflammation at the root tip stimulates dormant cells in the area to form a cyst. The tooth may have been painful in the past, or may simply have darkened over time. A similar <em>residual cyst</em> can remain in the bone after such a tooth has been extracted.</p>
<h3>Dentigerous cyst</h3>
<p>A dentigerous cyst forms around the crown of a tooth that has not erupted, most often a lower wisdom tooth or an upper canine. It is the second most common type and is frequently discovered when an X-ray is taken to plan wisdom tooth removal — see our article on <a href="/blogs/symptoms-of-impacted-wisdom-tooth/">impacted wisdom tooth symptoms</a>. In children, a dentigerous cyst around a developing tooth can sometimes be managed in a way that still allows the tooth to erupt.</p>
<h3>Odontogenic keratocyst (OKC)</h3>
<p>The keratocyst behaves differently. Its lining is thin and fragile, it produces a thick, cheesy material called keratin, and it tends to grow along the length of the bone rather than expanding outwards — commonly in the back of the lower jaw and the ramus (the upright part of the jaw behind the teeth). Keratocysts have a well-recognised tendency to recur after removal, and for some years they were classified as a tumour. People with several keratocysts, especially at a young age, may be assessed for <strong>Gorlin syndrome</strong> (naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome), an inherited condition that also affects the skin and skeleton.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Where it usually forms</th><th>Typical cause</th><th>Tendency to recur</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Radicular</td><td>Root tip of a tooth with a dead nerve</td><td>Chronic inflammation after decay or injury</td><td>Low once the cause is treated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dentigerous</td><td>Around the crown of an unerupted tooth</td><td>Fluid collecting around a buried tooth</td><td>Low after complete removal</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Odontogenic keratocyst</td><td>Back of the lower jaw and ramus, but can occur anywhere</td><td>Developmental; sometimes linked to Gorlin syndrome</td><td>Higher; needs longer follow-up</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="diagnosis">How a jaw cyst is diagnosed</h2>
<p>Diagnosis combines examination, imaging and — crucially — analysis of the tissue itself.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Clinical examination:</strong> checking the swelling, the teeth, the bite and the sensation of the lip and chin. Teeth near the cyst are tested to see whether their nerve is still alive (vitality testing).</li>
  <li><strong>Panoramic X-ray (OPG):</strong> the familiar wide dental X-ray, which shows most cysts as a dark, well-defined area in the bone.</li>
  <li><strong>CBCT or CT scan:</strong> a three-dimensional scan that shows the exact size of the cyst, how thin the bone has become and its relationship to the nerve, the sinus and neighbouring roots. This is essential for planning surgery on larger lesions.</li>
  <li><strong>Aspiration or biopsy:</strong> drawing off fluid with a fine needle, or removing a small piece of the lining, helps distinguish a simple cyst from a keratocyst or a tumour before major surgery is planned.</li>
</ul>
<p>The final diagnosis always comes from the laboratory report on the tissue removed. Until then, any label based on an X-ray alone is a working diagnosis.</p>

