<h2 id="treatment">Enucleation vs marsupialisation</h2>
<p>Small radicular cysts sometimes heal after root canal treatment alone, because removing the source of inflammation allows the bone to repair. When that is not enough, or when the cyst is of another type, surgery is the standard treatment. There are two main approaches.</p>
<h3>Enucleation</h3>
<p>The surgeon opens the gum, makes a window in the thinned bone and lifts out the entire cyst lining, ideally in one piece. The associated tooth is dealt with at the same time — a buried tooth is usually removed, while a tooth with a dead nerve may need root canal treatment or extraction. The cavity fills with a blood clot that gradually turns into new bone over the following months. Most cavities heal without the need for a bone graft.</p>
<p>For keratocysts, the surgeon may add measures to lower the risk of recurrence, such as carefully removing a thin layer of the surrounding bone or treating the cavity wall with a chemical solution. Evidence on which of these adjuncts works most reliably is still mixed, and the choice depends on the size and position of the cyst.</p>
<h3>Marsupialisation (decompression)</h3>
<p>For a very large cyst — especially one close to the nerve, the floor of the nose or the maxillary sinus, or one so big that removing it in one go could weaken the jaw — the surgeon may first create a small opening and stitch the cyst lining to the inside of the mouth, sometimes keeping it open with a small tube or plug. This releases the pressure. Over several months the cyst shrinks and new bone forms around it. Many patients then have a second, smaller operation to remove what remains.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Enucleation</th><th>Marsupialisation</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Number of stages</td><td>Usually one</td><td>Often two (decompression, then removal)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Suited to</td><td>Small to moderate cysts</td><td>Large cysts near the nerve, the sinus or developing teeth</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tissue for analysis</td><td>Complete specimen at the first operation</td><td>A sample first; the complete specimen at the second stage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Aftercare</td><td>Routine healing checks</td><td>Daily rinsing of the opening for several months</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main advantage</td><td>Everything is removed at once</td><td>Protects nearby nerves, teeth and bone</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Small cysts can often be removed under local anaesthesia. Larger cysts are treated under general anaesthesia in hospital, usually as a day-care or short-stay procedure. When hospital-based surgery is medically necessary, health insurance frequently contributes, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation.</p>

<h2 id="risks-recovery">Risks, recovery and follow-up imaging</h2>
<p>Recovery varies with the size of the cyst and the procedure. After enucleation of a moderate cyst, most people have swelling and discomfort for a few days, eat a soft diet for about a week and return to desk work within a few days to a week. Stitches dissolve or are removed within one to two weeks.</p>
<p>As with any surgery, there are risks to understand before you decide:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Numbness of the lip, chin or gum</strong> if the cyst lies against a nerve. This is usually temporary but can occasionally be long-lasting.</li>
  <li><strong>Infection</strong> of the cavity, treated with cleaning and sometimes antibiotics.</li>
  <li><strong>Effects on neighbouring teeth</strong>, which may later need root canal treatment.</li>
  <li><strong>An opening into the sinus or nose</strong> after removal of large upper-jaw cysts, which usually heals on its own but sometimes needs repair.</li>
  <li><strong>Jaw fracture</strong> — uncommon, but a risk when a very large cyst has thinned the lower jaw. You may be asked to avoid hard foods for a period.</li>
  <li><strong>Recurrence</strong>, most relevant to keratocysts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Follow-up imaging matters.</strong> Bone healing is checked on X-rays, typically a few months after surgery and then at intervals. For radicular and dentigerous cysts, one or two reassuring scans are often enough. For keratocysts, many surgeons advise periodic X-rays for at least five years, and longer for people with Gorlin syndrome, because recurrences can appear late and are simpler to manage when they are small.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Ask your dentist to refer you to an oral and maxillofacial surgeon if you have:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A dark area in the jawbone noticed on a routine dental X-ray</li>
  <li>A slowly enlarging, firm swelling of the jaw or gum</li>
  <li>Teeth that have shifted or loosened without gum disease</li>
  <li>A tooth that has not erupted when expected</li>
  <li>Numbness of the lower lip or chin (seek assessment promptly)</li>
  <li>A swelling that discharges fluid or keeps getting infected</li>
</ul>
<p>If the area becomes rapidly swollen, hot and painful, or you develop fever or difficulty swallowing, treat it as a possible spreading infection and read our guide on <a href="/blogs/facial-swelling-from-tooth-infection-emergency-signs/">when facial swelling is an emergency</a>.</p>
